\section{Parsing and formatting}

Internally every time is an integer number of minutes since midnight.
Comparing and sorting integers is trivial, whereas comparing [[HH:MM]]
strings only works by accident of zero-padding, and arithmetic on them
does not work at all.  The working day is therefore two constants in
the same unit.

<<constants>>=
DAY_START = 8 * 60
DAY_END = 17 * 60
@

A time is converted by splitting at the colon.  We reject anything that
is not a valid clock time, so that a typo such as [[9:75]] fails loudly
instead of silently shifting a booking.

<<functions>>=
def parse_time(text):
    """Return minutes since midnight for a time like `09:30`."""
    hours, sep, minutes = text.strip().partition(":")
    if not (sep and hours.isdigit() and minutes.isdigit()):
        raise ValueError(f"bad time: {text!r}")
    h, m = int(hours), int(minutes)
    if h > 23 or m > 59:
        raise ValueError(f"bad time: {text!r}")
    return h * 60 + m


def format_time(minutes):
    """Return `HH:MM` for a number of minutes since midnight."""
    return f"{minutes // 60:02d}:{minutes % 60:02d}"
@

A slot is a pair of such times separated by a hyphen.  A slot that ends
before it starts cannot be a real booking, so it is an error rather than
something we guess a meaning for.

<<functions>>=
def parse_slot(line):
    """Return `(start, end)` in minutes for a line like `09:00-10:30`."""
    start_text, sep, end_text = line.partition("-")
    if not sep:
        raise ValueError(f"bad slot: {line!r}")
    start, end = parse_time(start_text), parse_time(end_text)
    if end < start:
        raise ValueError(f"slot ends before it starts: {line!r}")
    return start, end


def format_slot(slot):
    """Return `HH:MM-HH:MM` for a `(start, end)` pair of minutes."""
    return f"{format_time(slot[0])}-{format_time(slot[1])}"
@

Let's verify that parsing and formatting are inverse to each other and
that malformed input is rejected:

<<test functions>>=
def test_parse_and_format_roundtrip():
  assert parse_slot("09:00-10:30") == (540, 630)
  assert format_slot((540, 630)) == "09:00-10:30"


def test_parse_rejects_malformed_input():
  for bad in ["", "09:00", "9:75-10:00", "ab:cd-10:00", "10:00-09:00"]:
    with pytest.raises(ValueError):
      parse_slot(bad)
@

\section{Merging bookings}

Bookings may overlap, touch, or arrive in any order, which makes them
awkward to subtract from the day one at a time.  If we first sort them by
start time, one left-to-right sweep suffices: a booking either begins
after everything seen so far has ended, and so opens a new busy interval,
or it begins at or before the current interval's end, and so can only
extend it.  Sorting is what makes this true: no later booking can start
before the current one, so no gap can ever be reopened behind the sweep.

Using [[<=]] rather than [[<]] when comparing the start to the current
end also merges bookings that merely touch.  That is harmless for our
purpose, since touching bookings leave no free time between them, and it
keeps the result a list of disjoint intervals.

<<functions>>=
def merge_slots(slots):
    """Return the sorted, disjoint union of `(start, end)` slots."""
    merged = []
    for start, end in sorted(slots):
        if merged and start <= merged[-1][1]:
            merged[-1] = (merged[-1][0], max(merged[-1][1], end))
        else:
            merged.append((start, end))
    return merged
@

The [[max]] matters when a long booking contains a shorter one, as in
[[09:00-12:00]] and [[10:00-11:00]]: the second must not shrink the
interval.  Let's verify that case along with overlap, touching and
unordered input:

<<test functions>>=
def test_merge_overlapping_touching_and_unordered():
  slots = [(600, 660), (540, 600), (630, 700), (900, 960)]
  assert merge_slots(slots) == [(540, 700), (900, 960)]


def test_merge_nested_booking_does_not_shrink():
  assert merge_slots([(540, 720), (600, 660)]) == [(540, 720)]
@

\section{Computing the free slots}

The free time is the complement of the busy intervals within the working
day.  We walk through the merged intervals keeping a [[cursor]] for the
start of the next possibly free minute.  Whenever an interval starts
after the cursor, the stretch in between is free.  Bookings are clamped
to the working day first: a booking at 07:00--09:00 only occupies
08:00--09:00 of it, and one entirely outside the day is dropped, because
clamping would otherwise turn it into an empty or inverted slot.

<<functions>>=
def free_slots(bookings, day_start=DAY_START, day_end=DAY_END):
    """Return the free `(start, end)` slots of the day, in order."""
    clamped = [
        (max(start, day_start), min(end, day_end))
        for start, end in bookings
        if end > day_start and start < day_end
    ]
    free = []
    cursor = day_start
    for start, end in merge_slots(clamped):
        if start > cursor:
            free.append((cursor, start))
        cursor = max(cursor, end)
    if cursor < day_end:
        free.append((cursor, day_end))
    return free
@

Let's verify the boundary cases: an empty calendar leaves the whole day
free, a fully booked day leaves nothing, bookings at the edges of the
day leave no empty slots, and bookings outside the day are ignored.

<<test functions>>=
def test_free_slots_empty_calendar():
  assert free_slots([]) == [(480, 1020)]


def test_free_slots_between_bookings():
  booked = [(parse_slot("13:00-14:00")), parse_slot("09:00-10:30")]
  assert [format_slot(s) for s in free_slots(booked)] == [
    "08:00-09:00", "10:30-13:00", "14:00-17:00"]


def test_free_slots_fully_booked():
  assert free_slots([(480, 1020)]) == []


def test_free_slots_bookings_at_edges():
  assert free_slots([(480, 540), (960, 1020)]) == [(540, 960)]


def test_free_slots_outside_day_ignored():
  assert free_slots([(0, 480), (1020, 1200)]) == [(480, 1020)]
  assert free_slots([(420, 540)]) == [(540, 1020)]
@

\section{The command-line entry point}

The entry point reads all of standard input, skips blank lines so that a
trailing newline or a spacer line is not an error, and prints one free
slot per line.  The pure function [[free_slots_from_lines]] does the work
so that tests can exercise it without touching real standard input.

<<functions>>=
def free_slots_from_lines(lines):
    """Return free slots as `HH:MM-HH:MM` strings for booking lines."""
    bookings = [parse_slot(line.strip()) for line in lines if line.strip()]
    return [format_slot(slot) for slot in free_slots(bookings)]
@

<<main function>>=
def main():
    """Read bookings from stdin and print the free slots."""
    for slot in free_slots_from_lines(sys.stdin):
        print(slot)
@

Finally we check the whole pipeline through [[main]], with overlapping,
touching and out-of-order bookings on the input:

<<test functions>>=
def test_main_end_to_end(monkeypatch, capsys):
  text = "13:00-14:00\n09:00-10:00\n09:30-10:30\n10:30-11:00\n\n"
  monkeypatch.setattr("sys.stdin", io.StringIO(text))
  main()
  assert capsys.readouterr().out.splitlines() == [
    "08:00-09:00", "11:00-13:00", "14:00-17:00"]
@
